Notebook[{Cell["Advanced Lesson 13 - Exercises: Trace and Held Evaluation\nMikey Ferguson\nMATH 7013 - Advanced Mathematical Modeling", "Title"], Cell["1: Tracing differentiation and integration", "Section"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{RowBox[{"Clear", "[", "x", "]"}], ";", "\[IndentingNewLine]", RowBox[{"Trace", "[", RowBox[{SubscriptBox["\[PartialD]", RowBox[{"x"}]], SuperscriptBox["x", "2"]}], "]"}]}]], "Input"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"Trace", "[", RowBox[{SubscriptBox["\[PartialD]", RowBox[{"x"}]], SuperscriptBox[RowBox[{"(", RowBox[{"1", "+", "x"}], ")"}], "2"]}], "]"}]], "Input"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"Trace", "[", RowBox[{SubscriptBox["\[PartialD]", RowBox[{"x"}]], SuperscriptBox[RowBox[{"(", RowBox[{"x", "+", "1"}], ")"}], "2"]}], "]"}]], "Input"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"Trace", "[", RowBox[{"\[Integral]", RowBox[{RowBox[{"(", RowBox[{"x", "+", SuperscriptBox["x", "2"], "+", SuperscriptBox["\[ExponentialE]", "x"]}], ")"}], RowBox[{"\[DifferentialD]", "x"}]}]}], "]"}]], "Input"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"Trace", "[", RowBox[{SubscriptBox["\[PartialD]", RowBox[{"x"}]], RowBox[{"\[Integral]", RowBox[{SuperscriptBox[RowBox[{"(", RowBox[{"1", "+", "x"}], ")"}], "2"], RowBox[{"\[DifferentialD]", "x"}]}]}]}], "]"}]], "Input"], Cell["What the traces show. Trace returns a nested list of the intermediate\nexpressions Mathematica passed through, with the nesting recording how deep\nin the evaluation each step occurred. By default it reports only the steps\nthat change something, which is why these traces are so much shorter than\nthe calculations they describe - D and Integrate do their work inside\nbuilt-in code that Trace does not open up.\nThe first trace has two entries: the input D[x^2, x] and the result 2 x.\nThe second, for (1 + x)^2, is the same shape - input and result 2 (1 + x).\nThe third, for (x + 1)^2, is the interesting one. It has an extra step at\nthe very front: x + 1 becoming 1 + x. Plus is Orderless, so the argument was\nsorted into canonical order before D was ever reached, and after that first\nline the trace is identical to the second. The way a sum is typed survives\nexactly one step and then vanishes.\nThe fourth trace shows the same thing again - x + x^2 + E^x is reordered to\nE^x + x + x^2 first - and then the integral appears in one step as\nE^x + x^2/2 + x^3/3. The linearity is not visible, because it happens inside\nIntegrate rather than as separate evaluation steps.\nThe fifth is the most instructive. The trace shows Integrate[(1 + x)^2, x]\nevaluated all the way to x + x^2 + x^3/3, and only then D applied to that\nresult, giving 1 + 2 x + x^2. Mathematica does not notice that\ndifferentiation undoes integration and short-circuit the pair; it evaluates\ninnermost first, because that is what ordinary evaluation order requires.\nThe final answer is the expanded polynomial rather than the original\n(1 + x)^2, since the intermediate antiderivative came back expanded.", "Text"], Cell["2: Tracing immediate against delayed replacement rules", "Section"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{RowBox[{"Clear", "[", RowBox[{"a", ",", "b"}], "]"}], ";", "\[IndentingNewLine]", RowBox[{"Trace", "[", RowBox[{RowBox[{"Sin", "[", "a", "]"}], "/.", "\[VeryThinSpace]", RowBox[{"a", "\[Rule]", "b"}]}], "]"}]}]], "Input"], Cell["Part 1. With neither symbol assigned, the trace is almost empty: the rule\nmatches, a becomes b, and Sin[b] is returned. Nothing else happens.", "Text"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{RowBox[{"Clear", "[", RowBox[{"a", ",", "b"}], "]"}], ";", "\[IndentingNewLine]", RowBox[{"b", "=", "5"}], ";", "\[IndentingNewLine]", RowBox[{"Trace", "[", RowBox[{RowBox[{"Sin", "[", "a", "]"}], "/.", "\[VeryThinSpace]", RowBox[{"a", "\[Rule]", "b"}]}], "]"}]}]], "Input"], Cell["Part 2. The rule a -> b is an immediate rule, so its right-hand side is\nevaluated before the replacement is attempted. The trace shows b evaluating\nto 5 first, so the rule that actually gets applied is a -> 5, and the result\nis Sin[5].", "Text"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{RowBox[{"Clear", "[", RowBox[{"a", ",", "b"}], "]"}], ";", "\[IndentingNewLine]", RowBox[{"a", "=", "2"}], ";", "\[IndentingNewLine]", RowBox[{"b", "=", "5"}], ";", "\[IndentingNewLine]", RowBox[{"Trace", "[", RowBox[{RowBox[{"Sin", "[", "a", "]"}], "/.", "\[VeryThinSpace]", RowBox[{"a", "\[Rule]", "b"}]}], "]"}]}]], "Input"], Cell["Part 3. Now both sides evaluate before ReplaceAll runs. The expression\nSin[a] becomes Sin[2] and the rule becomes 2 -> 5, which still matches, so\nthe answer is Sin[5]. The trace shows both evaluations happening ahead of\nthe replacement. Note the accident: it only worked because the rule's\nleft-hand side evaluated to the same thing the expression did.", "Text"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{RowBox[{"Clear", "[", RowBox[{"a", ",", "b"}], "]"}], ";", "\[IndentingNewLine]", RowBox[{"Trace", "[", RowBox[{RowBox[{"Sin", "[", "a", "]"}], "/.", "\[VeryThinSpace]", RowBox[{"a", "\[RuleDelayed]", "b"}]}], "]"}]}]], "Input"], Cell["Part 4. With nothing assigned, RuleDelayed behaves exactly like Rule and the\ntrace is the same. The difference between them is invisible until the\nright-hand side has something to evaluate.", "Text"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{RowBox[{"Clear", "[", RowBox[{"a", ",", "b"}], "]"}], ";", "\[IndentingNewLine]", RowBox[{"b", "=", "5"}], ";", "\[IndentingNewLine]", RowBox[{"Trace", "[", RowBox[{RowBox[{"Sin", "[", "a", "]"}], "/.", "\[VeryThinSpace]", RowBox[{"a", "\[RuleDelayed]", "b"}]}], "]"}]}]], "Input"], Cell["Part 5. Here the two diverge. RuleDelayed holds its right-hand side, so b is\nnot evaluated when the rule is constructed. It is substituted as the symbol\nb, and only then, as part of the ordinary evaluation of the result, does b\nyield 5. The final answer is again Sin[5], but the trace reaches it by a\ndifferent route - the evaluation of b appears after the replacement instead\nof before it.", "Text"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{RowBox[{"Clear", "[", RowBox[{"a", ",", "b"}], "]"}], ";", "\[IndentingNewLine]", RowBox[{"a", "=", "2"}], ";", "\[IndentingNewLine]", RowBox[{"b", "=", "5"}], ";", "\[IndentingNewLine]", RowBox[{"Trace", "[", RowBox[{RowBox[{"Sin", "[", "a", "]"}], "/.", "\[VeryThinSpace]", RowBox[{"a", "\[RuleDelayed]", "b"}]}], "]"}]}]], "Input"], Cell["Part 6. The result is Sin[5] once more.\nThe observation the exercise asks for. The left-hand side of a delayed rule\nis not held: a is evaluated to 2 in the rule just as it is in the\nexpression. But that makes no difference to the answer, because it happens\non both sides at once - the expression becomes Sin[2] and the rule becomes\n2 :> b, and they still match. Only the right-hand side is protected by\nRuleDelayed, and only the right-hand side needs to be, since that is where\nthe substituted material comes from.", "Text"], Cell["3: Displaying arguments unevaluated and evaluated, side by side", "Section"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{RowBox[{"ClearAll", "[", RowBox[{"f", ",", "a"}], "]"}], ";", "\[IndentingNewLine]", RowBox[{"SetAttributes", "[", RowBox[{"f", ",", "HoldAll"}], "]"}], ";", "\[IndentingNewLine]", RowBox[{RowBox[{"f", "[", "args___", "]"}], ":=", RowBox[{"List", "@@", RowBox[{RowBox[{"Function", "[", RowBox[{"u", ",", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{RowBox[{"HoldForm", "[", "u", "]"}], ",", "u"}], "}"}], ",", "HoldAll"}], "]"}], "/@", RowBox[{"Hold", "[", "args", "]"}]}]}]}]}]], "Input"], Cell["How this works, since the exercise offers no hints.\nThe function must see its arguments before they are evaluated, so it is\ngiven the HoldAll attribute. Inside, the held sequence is captured by a\nBlankNullSequence and immediately wrapped: Hold[args] is a Hold expression\ncontaining all of the arguments, still unevaluated.\nMap applies a function to each element of that Hold at level one. The\nfunction being mapped is itself a pure function declared HoldAll, which is\nthe essential trick - a plain pure function would evaluate its argument on\nthe way in and the unevaluated form would be lost before anything could\nrecord it. Note that this is Function with a named parameter and a third\nargument giving attributes, not the ampersand form; the ampersand form has\nno place to put HoldAll.\nFor each argument the mapped function builds a two-element list: HoldForm[u],\nwhich keeps the argument in its original form for display, and a bare u,\nwhich is evaluated in the ordinary way as the body evaluates. One argument,\ntwo renderings.\nFinally Apply replaces the outer Hold with List, releasing the structure into\nan ordinary list of pairs.", "Text"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{RowBox[{"Clear", "[", "a", "]"}], ";", "\[IndentingNewLine]", RowBox[{"f", "[", RowBox[{RowBox[{"N", "[", "\[Pi]", "]"}], ",", RowBox[{"1", "+", "2"}], ",", RowBox[{"a", " ", SuperscriptBox["a", "2"]}], ",", RowBox[{"a", " ", RowBox[{"(", RowBox[{"1", "+", "a"}], ")"}]}]}], "]"}]}]], "Input"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"TableForm", "[", RowBox[{"f", "[", RowBox[{RowBox[{"N", "[", "\[Pi]", "]"}], ",", RowBox[{"1", "+", "2"}], ",", RowBox[{"a", " ", SuperscriptBox["a", "2"]}], ",", RowBox[{"a", " ", RowBox[{"(", RowBox[{"1", "+", "a"}], ")"}]}]}], "]"}], "]"}]], "Input"], Cell["The result is\n{{N[Pi], 3.141592653589793}, {1 + 2, 3}, {a a^2, a^3}, {a (1 + a), a (1 + a)}},\nmatching the target output in the exercise, and TableForm lays it out as\nfour rows of two columns.\nThe fourth row is the one that shows the mechanism is honest: a (1 + a)\nappears identically in both columns, because Mathematica does not expand\nproducts of sums on its own. It is not that the evaluated column failed -\nthat expression really is its own evaluated form. The third row is the\ncontrast: a a^2 is collected into a^3 by the automatic rules for Times, so\nthe two columns differ.", "Text"]}, WindowSize -> {1100, 850}, StyleDefinitions -> "Default.nb"]
(* End of Notebook Content *)
